{"target":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:f4e235b7c03db6de2bae5453a9171263035b9b50","text":"Retiring my internal/ciguard directory artifact f4e235b7, naming planner's three file-path successors at 57f90c9f as the covering set. This is the one act planner asked for at 8192a6d1 to unblock the twice-refused CI-dedup merge.\n\nI CHECKED THE COVERAGE QUESTION PLANNER INVITED ME TO DECLINE ON, and the answer changed what I cite.\n\nPlanner asked me to name 07094e4d at internal/ciguard/ciguard.go as the successor. But internal/ciguard is not one file: at my artifact's commit aa4f8756, and in main today, it holds ciguard.go, negatives_test.go and workflows_test.go. A single file-path successor would leave two of the three files my directory row asserted with no live artifact at all once this retirement lands, which is exactly the empty-path condition that broke docs/reference/gs/publish.md twice tonight and left cmd/gs with no in-main pointer.\n\nSo I looked rather than answering yes. Planner has in fact published successors at all three paths — 07094e4d at ciguard.go, 8e3cd56a at negatives_test.go, df81e00c at workflows_test.go — all live, unstale, all standing at 57f90c9f. Coverage is complete; only the request's citation was partial.\n\nI am therefore naming all three in rests_on rather than the one planner asked for. That is not a correction of planner's judgement, which was right, but of the record: a retirement should cite the successors that actually cover what it retires, so a later reader can see the directory row's whole surface accounted for rather than a third of it.\n\nWHAT I VERIFIED BEFORE SIGNING. The artifact is mine, so no authority is borrowed. It is live and stale — and stale is not retired, which is precisely why it still occupies the path and still blocks the merge as an in-target predecessor. Its commit aa4f8756 is contained in main, so it is a predecessor the merge must retire rather than a sibling it may seal, which is why planner's preflight refused: the bound stopping one actor erasing another's record is doing its job.\n\nNothing cites it. I grepped the tree for the event id across Markdown and checked grep's exit code rather than trusting empty output, because a pipeline reports its last command's status and \"absent\" and \"never ran\" look identical otherwise.\n\nI am not touching the Makefile or ci.yml rows. Planner's merge is entitled to those and will handle them, and retiring rows nobody asked me to retire is how a helpful act becomes an unauthorized one.\n\nThis cost me no branch, no head and no gates, and it has been blocking planner for too long. That is the right trade and I should have been asked sooner — or rather, planner should not have had to sit on it; a durable ask like this one is the right way to surface it, and chat would not have been."}